s","description":"Jamaican politician; 9th Prime Minister of Jamaica","enwiki":"Andrew Holness","summary":"Andrew Michael Holness,  MP (born 22 July 1972) is a Jamaican politician who has served as Prime Minister of Jamaica since 3 March 2016, having previously served from 2011 to 2012, and as leader of the Jamaica Labour Party (JLP) since 2011.\nHolness previously served as prime minister from 23 October 2011 to 5 January 2012. He succeeded Bruce Golding as prime minister and decided to go to the polls in the 29 December 2011 general election in an attempt to get his own mandate from the Jamaican electorate. He failed in that bid, however, losing to the People's National Party led by Portia Simpson-Miller, with the PNP gaining 42 seats to the JLP's 21.","image":"Andrew Holness Press (cropped).jpg","birth":1972.5546448087432,"death":null},"Q9682":{"label":"Elizabeth II","description":"Queen of the United Kingdom from 1952 to 2022 (1926–2022)","enwiki":"Elizabeth II","summary":"Elizabeth II (Elizabeth Alexandra Mary; 21 April 1926 – 8 September 2022) was Queen of the United Kingdom and other Commonwealth realms from 6 February 1952 until her death in 2022. She was queen regnant of 32 sovereign states during her lifetime and was the monarch of 15 realms at her death. Her reign of 70 years and 214 days is the longest of any British monarch, the second-longest of any sovereign state, and the longest of any queen regnant in history.\nElizabeth was born in Mayfair, London, during the reign of her paternal grandfather, King George V. She was the first child of the Duke and Duchess of York (later King George VI and Queen Elizabeth the Queen Mother).","image":"Queen Elizabeth II official portrait for 1959 tour (retouched) (cropped) (3-to-4 aspect ratio).jpg","birth":1926.3013698630136,"death":2022.6849315068494},"Q109147":{"label":"Patrick Allen","description":"Jamaican politician, current Governor-General of Jamaica","enwiki":"Patrick Allen (governor-general)","summary":"Sir Patrick Linton Allen  (born 7 February 1951) is a Jamaican statesman and former Seventh-day Adventist pastor, who is serving as the sixth and current governor-general of Jamaica since 26 February 2009.\nThe fourth of five children in a family of subsistence farmers, Allen spent over a decade as a teacher and principal, before leaving education to be trained as an Adventist minister at Andrews University in the United States. After becoming a pastor, Allen went on to serve in a number of leadership roles within the Seventh-day Adventist Church, eventually becoming the leader of the West Indies Union of Seventh-day Adventists, which had jurisdiction over Jamaica, The Bahamas, the Cayman Islands, and the Turks and Caicos Islands.\nAllen became Jamaica's sixth appointed governor-general (eighth overall, including two acting governors-general).","image":"Patrick Allen Coronation 2023.jpg","birth":1951.1013698630136,"death":null},"Q1294179":{"label":"Edward Zacca","description":"Jamaican judge (1931–2019)","enwiki":"Edward Zacca","summary":"Sir Edward Zacca   (26 July 1931 – 11 November 2019) was chief justice of the Jamaican Supreme Court from 1985 to 1996. Under the Constitution of Jamaica, the chief justice of Jamaica serves as acting governor-general of Jamaica when that office is vacant, and Chief Justice Zacca served in that capacity from March to August 1991. Zacca was succeeded as Chief Justice by the Hon. Mr.","image":null,"birth":1931.5643835616438,"death":2019.8602739726027},"Q1371514":{"label":"Howard Cooke","description":"Jamaican politician (1915-2014)","enwiki":"Howard Cooke","summary":"Sir Howard Felix Hanlan Cooke   (13 November 1915 – 11 July 2014) served as the fourth Governor-General of Jamaica from 1 August 1991 to 15 February 2006.","image":"Howard-cooke.jpg","birth":1915.8657534246574,"death":2014.5232876712328},"Q1430193":{"label":"Florizel Glasspole","description":"Jamaican politician (1909-2000)","enwiki":"Florizel Glasspole","summary":"Sir Florizel Augustus Glasspole   (25 September 1909 – 25 November 2000) was the third and longest-serving governor-general of Jamaica, in office from 1973 to 1991.","image":"Sir Florizel Glasspole (cropped).jpg","birth":1909.731506849315,"death":2000.8989071038252},"Q3366368":{"label":"Kenneth Blackburne","description":"British colonial administrator (1907–1980)","enwiki":"Kenneth Blackburne","summary":"Sir Kenneth William Blackburne  (12 December 1907 – 4 November 1980) was a British colonial official who was the first governor-general of Jamaica. He was knighted in 1952.","image":null,"birth":1907.945205479452,"death":1980.8415300546449},"Q339040":{"label":"Clifford Campbell","description":"Jamaican politician (1892-1991)","enwiki":"Clifford Campbell","summary":"Sir Clifford Clarence Campbell   (28 June 1892 – 28 September 1991) was a Jamaican educator and politician who served as speaker of the House and President of the Senate. In 1962, after Jamaica achieved independence, he was appointed as the first Jamaica-born  and second governor-general of Jamaica, serving in that position for more than a decade.","image":"Clifford Campbell.jpg","birth":1892.4890710382513,"death":1991.7397260273972},"Q3511502":{"label":"Herbert Duffus","description":"Jamaican politician and judge (1908-2002)","enwiki":"Herbert Duffus","summary":"Sir Herbert George Holwell Duffus (30 August 1908 – 25 October 2002) was a Jamaican who served as the chief Justice of Jamaica and as acting governor-general of Jamaica.","image":null,"birth":1908.6612021857923,"death":2002.813698630137},"Q548609":{"label":"Kenneth O. Hall","description":"Governor-General of Jamaica","enwiki":"Kenneth O. Hall","summary":"Sir Kenneth Octavius Hall  (born 24 April 1941) is a former diplomat who served as the governor-general of Jamaica from 16 February 2006 to 26 February 2009. He was Jamaica's fifth governor-general since independence in 1962.","image":null,"birth":1941.309589041096,"death":null}},"media":{"Flag of Jamaica (1906–1957).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Flag_of_Jamaica_%281906%E2%80%931957%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_Jamaica_%281906%E2%80%931957%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Jamaica_(1906%E2%80%931957).svg","author":"Thommy","license":"Public domain"},"Flag of Jamaica (1962).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Flag_of_Jamaica_%281962%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_Jamaica_%281962%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Jamaica_(1962).svg","author":"Thommy","license":"Public domain"},"Flag of Jamaica (1957–1962).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Flag_of_Jamaica_%281957%E2%80%931962%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_Jamaica_%281957%E2%80%931962%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Jamaica_(1957%E2%80%931962).svg","author":"Thommy","license":"Public domain"},"Banner of arms of Jamaica.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Banner_of_arms_of_Jamaica.svg/330px-Banner_of_arms_of_Jamaica.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_arms_of_Jamaica.svg","author":"Own work based on: Flag of England.svg:  and Jamaica Coat of Arms.svg:","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},"Flag of Jamaica.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Flag_of_Jamaica.svg/330px-Flag_of_Jamaica.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Jamaica.svg","author":"The SVG code is valid. This vector image was created with Inkscape by SKopp, and then manually edite